Length)`. The first call checks if the new data has stable updates compared to the existing data. The second call checks if the existing data has stable updates compared to the new data.\n\nThe vulnerability is in the first call: `IsStableUpdated(aTlvs, aTlvsLength, mTlvs, mLength)`. This compares the new data (attacker-controlled) with the existing data.\n\nIf the new data has a Prefix TLV with a BorderRouter sub-TLV that has a large length, and the existing data has the same prefix with a BorderRouter sub-TLV that has a smaller length, the `memcmp` reads past the end of the existing data's BorderRouter TLV.\n\nBut wait, the `rlocIn` flag is set based on `RlocLookup(aRloc16, rlocIn, rlocStable, aTlvs, aTlvsLength, kMatchModeRloc16, false)`. This checks if the RLOC16 is in the new data. If it is, `rlocIn` is true.\n\nThen `RlocLookup(aRloc16, rlocIn, unused, mTlvs, mLength, kMatchModeRloc16)` checks if the RLOC16 is in the existing data. If it is, `rlocIn` remains true.\n\nSo the attack requires:\n1. The RLOC16 is in both the new data and the existing data\n2. The new data has a Prefix TLV with a BorderRouter sub-TLV that has a large length\n3. The existing data has the same prefix with a BorderRouter sub-TLV that has a smaller length\n\nBut how do we set up the existing data? The existing data is the leader's network data. Initially, after `otThreadBecomeLeader`, the leader's network data might be empty.\n\nSo we need to first register some network data with a prefix and a BorderRouter TLV, and then register new network data with the same prefix but a BorderRouter TLV with a larger length.\n\nBut both registrations happen via the same mechanism. The first registration sets up the existing data. The second registration triggers the vulnerability.\n\nBut the fuzzer only sends one UART input. So we need to craft a single input that:\n1. First, sends a spinel command to set up the initial network data with a prefix and a small BorderRouter TLV\n2. Then, sends another s