ocesses the data and may generate multiple spinel commands. Each spinel command may trigger a CoAP message to be sent to the leader.\\n\\nBut the key insight is that the `otPlatUartReceived` function is called once, and then the tasklets are processed. During tasklet processing, the CoAP messages are delivered and processed.\\n\\nSo the attack is:\\n1. Send a spinel command to set the local network data with a crafted Prefix TLV containing a BorderRouter TLV with a large length\\n2. During tasklet processing, `SendServerDataNotification` is called, which sends a CoAP message to the leader\\n3. The leader's `HandleServerData` processes the CoAP message\\n4. `RegisterNetworkData` is called with the crafted TLVs\\n5. `IsStableUpdated` compares the crafted TLVs with the existing data\\n6. The `memcmp` reads past the end of the existing data's BorderRouter TLV\\n\\nBut wait, step 1 requires that the spinel command can set arbitrary TLVs. Let me check if the spinel command for setting on-mesh prefixes allows setting arbitrary BorderRouter TLV lengths.\\n\\nActually, looking at the spinel property format for `SPINEL_PROP_THREAD_ON_MESH_NETS`:\\n```\\nFormat: A(t(6CbCbS))\\n```\\n\\nThis is an array of structures with:\\n- `6`: IPv6 Prefix (16 bytes)\\n- `C`: Prefix length in bits\\n- `b`: Stable flag\\n- `C`: TLV flags\\n- `b`: \\\"Is defined locally\\\" flag\\n- `S`: RLOC16\\n\\nThis doesn't allow setting arbitrary TLV lengths. The BorderRouter TLV is constructed by the NCP based on the flags.\\n\\nSo we can't directly set arbitrary BorderRouter TLV lengths via spinel commands.\\n\\nLet me think about another approach. Maybe the vulnerability is triggered differently.\\n\\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the `HandleServerData` function:\\n\\n```cpp\\nvoid Leader::HandleServerData(Coap::Message &aMessage, const Ip6::MessageInfo &aMessageInfo)\\n{\\n    ThreadNetworkDataTlv networkData;\\n    ThreadRloc16Tlv      rloc16;\\n\\n    if (ThreadTlv::GetTlv(aMessage, ThreadTlv::kR